Umbra-Brass

Creative Catalyst: It descends from carnival musicians who perform only during solar eclipses.

This genre sounds like a high-altitude fever dream where gravity loses its grip. Imagine the frantic, jagged improvisations of a brass-led jazz-rock outfit played through speakers submerged in viscous mercury. The "Ethereal Jazz" element manifests as trumpet lines that disintegrate into digital rain, while the rhythm section—obsessed with irregular, circular movements—churns beneath a shroud of static and airy, cavernous reverb. It is music that feels oxygen-deprived, elegant, and perpetually mid-fall.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 65-110 BPM; erratic, "limping" odd-meter time signatures that fluctuate based on the perceived darkness of the sky.
*   Harmonic Palette: Primarily Microtonal Aeolian and Lydian dominant, characterized by heavy use of "shadow-intervals"—notes slightly flattened to mimic the psychological drop in temperature during an eclipse.
*   Production Signatures: 1) "Glass-Cabinet Isolation": recordings made in vacuum-sealed acoustic chambers; 2) "Reverse Tape Reverb-Trails" that appear before the brass hits; 3) "Lunar-Phase Compression," where the volume of the instruments subtly pulses in sync with tidal frequency algorithms.
*   Dynamics: Built on "Sudden Blinding Peaks"—whisper-quiet, atmospheric passages suddenly punctuated by violent, abrasive trumpet blasts that evaporate instantly into silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
The Umbra-Brass practitioners, known as The Penumbralists, hail from the coastal ruin of Ouroboros Harbor, a place that exists only on charts printed before the 1920s. They are nomadic performers who follow the path of totality across the globe, believing the sun’s temporary erasure opens a sonic portal to a sub-audible realm. Their fashion consists of moth-eaten velvet coats embroidered with maps of lunar cycles, their faces obscured by brass-filigree masks that act as acoustic modifiers for the trumpet. Their rituals involve "Sun-Binding," a pre-eclipse practice where they tether themselves to iron buoys and wait for the light to turn that sick, bruised purple, at which point the band begins a performance that can last for the entirety of the darkness, ending abruptly the moment the corona flickers back to life.

Central Instruments: The Aeolian Trumpet (a trumpet fitted with three extra sets of chromatic valves and a glass-bell), a modified analog modular synthesizer that generates "breath-currents," and bowed cymbals struck with rusted carnival-ride gears.

For Fans Of: Miles Davis (late Bitches Brew era), Arca, Don Ellis, Sigur Rós.

